  "client_view_text": " When a database crashes, the write-ahead log ensures that it can be recovered to a consistent state.\n## Step 1: Understanding the Role of the Write-Ahead Log\nA write-ahead log is a mechanism used by database systems to ensure data consistency and recovery in the event of a crash or failure. The log records all modifications to the database, including writes, updates, and deletes, before applying them to the actual database files. This ensures that any changes are persisted to the log before they are reflected in the database.\n\n## Step 2: The Role of the Write-Ahead Log in Crash Recovery\nWhen a database crashes, the + show your answer as text in a a code block.\n\n```\n# Write-Ahead Logging in a database\n\n# When a transaction is started, the database allocates a new log record\n# and stores the current checkpoint position in the log.\n\nstart_transaction() {\n  # Allocate a new log record\n  log_record = New LogRecord()\n\n  # Store the current checkpoint position in the log\n  log_record.checkpoint_position = current_checkpoint_position\n}\n\n# When a write operation is performed, the database writes the data\n# to the log first, and then writes it to the database.\n\nwrite(data) {\n  # Get an empty log record\n  log_record = GetEmptyLogRecord()\n\n  # Write the data to the log record\n  log_record.data = data\n\n  # Write the log record to the log\n  write_log(log_record)\n\n  # Update the database with the data\n  update_database(data)\n}\n\n# When the transaction is committed, the database updates the log to\n# reflect that the transaction is complete.\n\ncommit_transaction(transaction_id) {\n  # Set the transaction status in the log\n  set_transaction_status(transaction_id, \"COMMITTED\")\n\n  # Update the checkpoint position in the log\n  log.checkpoint_position = transaction"
}